ABSTRACT
Crime and emergency situations require timely reporting and rapid response mechanisms to prevent loss of lives and property. Traditional crime reporting methods commonly used in many police stations involve physical visits, telephone calls, and manual documentation processes, which often result in delays, poor record management, and ineffective emergency response coordination. These limitations hinder the ability of law enforcement agencies to respond efficiently to incidents and emergencies. This project presents the design and implementation of a Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division. The system was developed to provide a digital platform that enables citizens to report crimes and emergencies directly from their mobile devices while allowing police administrators to monitor, manage, and respond to incidents effectively. The system was developed using Expo (React Native) and TypeScript for the frontend, while Node.js, Express.js, and MongoDB were utilized for backend development and data management. Authentication and authorization were implemented using JSON Web Tokens (JWT), while Expo Location API was integrated to facilitate real-time location tracking of incident reports. Secure token storage was achieved through Expo Secure Store, and deployment was carried out using the Railway cloud platform. The proposed system provides features such as user registration, secure login, crime reporting, emergency alerts, GPS location sharing, incident management, administrative monitoring, and report history tracking. The integration of real-time location tracking enables law enforcement officers to identify incident locations accurately and respond more rapidly. The implementation and testing results demonstrate that the system significantly improves crime reporting efficiency, enhances emergency response coordination, reduces reporting delays, and strengthens communication between citizens and law enforcement agencies. The study concludes that the adoption of mobile-based crime reporting and emergency response systems can contribute significantly to public safety, operational efficiency, and effective crime management.
Keywords: Crime Reporting, Emergency Response System, Real-Time Tracking, GPS, React Native, Node.js, MongoDB, JWT Authentication, Public Safety.







 
CHAPTER ONE
INTRODUCTION
1.1 Background of the Study
Crime has become one of the most significant social challenges facing modern societies. Governments and security agencies continuously seek innovative methods to combat criminal activities and improve public safety. The effectiveness of crime prevention and control strategies depends largely on the availability of accurate information and timely responses to incidents. According to (Roberts, 2024), efficient crime reporting mechanisms serve as a critical link between citizens and law enforcement agencies because they facilitate the transmission of information required for investigations and emergency interventions. The rapid advancement of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) has transformed various sectors including healthcare, education, transportation, banking, and security. In recent years, mobile technologies, cloud computing, and location-based services have emerged as powerful tools for enhancing public safety operations (Garcia, 2021). Smartphones equipped with Global Positioning System (GPS) capabilities now provide opportunities for real-time communication and location sharing, enabling faster emergency response and improved situational awareness. Nigeria, like many developing countries, continues to face security challenges including armed robbery, kidnapping, terrorism, cybercrime, cultism, vandalism, and other forms of criminal activity. Effective crime management requires efficient reporting channels that enable citizens to communicate incidents promptly to law enforcement agencies. However, traditional reporting methods such as physical visits to police stations and telephone calls often result in delays and information inaccuracies (Johnson, 2020). The Nigerian Police Force is the primary institution responsible for maintaining law and order and ensuring public safety. Despite ongoing reforms and modernization efforts, many police stations still depend heavily on manual processes for crime reporting and emergency management. According to (Edwards, 2021), manual crime reporting systems are often characterized by poor record management, communication inefficiencies, and delayed response times. Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division, serves a large population within Auchi and neighboring communities in Edo State. The division handles various categories of crime reports and emergency incidents daily. Due to increasing urbanization and population growth, the demand for efficient crime reporting and emergency response services continues to rise. Traditional reporting mechanisms may no longer adequately support the operational requirements of modern policing. The emergence of mobile application technologies provides an opportunity to develop innovative solutions capable of addressing these challenges. React Native, Expo Framework, and TypeScript offer powerful tools for building cross-platform mobile applications that can facilitate instant communication between citizens and security agencies (Adams, 2021). Additionally, backend technologies such as Node.js, Express.js, and MongoDB provide scalable infrastructures for managing large volumes of data efficiently (Davis, 2020). The integration of location-based services into crime reporting systems offers significant advantages. Real-time location tracking enables emergency responders to identify incident locations accurately, thereby reducing response times and improving resource allocation (Clark, 2022). Such capabilities are particularly important during emergencies where delays may result in severe consequences. This project therefore proposes the development of a Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division. The system seeks to leverage modern software technologies to improve crime reporting processes, enhance emergency response effectiveness, strengthen communication between citizens and police officers, and contribute to safer communities.
1.2 Statement of the Problem
Despite technological advancements in communication systems, many police stations continue to rely on traditional methods for crime reporting and emergency response management. The existing crime reporting process often requires victims or witnesses to physically visit police stations before filing complaints. This approach can be inconvenient, time-consuming, and ineffective during emergencies. Another major problem is delayed emergency response. Citizens who call emergency numbers may not always provide accurate location details, making it difficult for law enforcement officers to identify and reach incident locations promptly (Clark, 2022). Manual documentation of crime reports also presents significant challenges. Physical records may become damaged, misplaced, duplicated, or difficult to retrieve when needed for investigations and decision-making processes (Green, 2020). Furthermore, communication barriers between citizens and police officers can discourage public participation in crime reporting. Many individuals may hesitate to report incidents due to lengthy procedures or concerns about response effectiveness. The absence of a centralized digital platform limits the ability of police administrators to monitor incidents, analyze crime patterns, and coordinate emergency responses effectively. Consequently, there is a need for a modern system capable of facilitating instant reporting, real-time location sharing, secure data storage, and efficient emergency response management.
1.3 Aim and Objectives of the Study
