1.7 Operational Definition of Terms
Crime Reporting: The process of informing law enforcement agencies about criminal activities.
Emergency Response: Immediate action taken to address urgent incidents threatening lives or property.
GPS: Global Positioning System used for determining geographic locations.
React Native: A framework used for developing mobile applications.
MongoDB: A NoSQL database management system.
JWT: JSON Web Token used for secure authentication.
Location Tracking: The process of identifying and monitoring the geographic position of a user or object in real time.
Citizen: A registered user who reports incidents using the system.
Administrator: An authorized police officer responsible for managing reports and system operations.
CHAPTER TWO
LITERATURE REVIEW
2.1 Introduction
Literature review involves the examination and evaluation of existing knowledge, theories, concepts, and empirical studies related to a particular research topic. It provides a foundation upon which new research can be built and helps researchers identify gaps in existing studies. This chapter reviews relevant literature related to crime reporting systems, emergency response systems, location-based services, real-time tracking technologies, mobile application development technologies, and related studies that support the development of the proposed Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division. The review is organized under conceptual review, theoretical framework, review of related studies, review of existing systems, identification of research gaps, and summary of the chapter.
2.2 Concept of Crime Reporting Systems
Crime reporting refers to the process through which citizens provide information regarding criminal activities or suspicious incidents to law enforcement agencies. Crime reporting systems are designed to facilitate communication between members of the public and security organizations to support crime prevention, investigation, and law enforcement operations. According to (Roberts, 2024), crime reporting systems serve as essential tools for public safety because they provide mechanisms through which citizens can share information that assists security agencies in detecting, preventing, and responding to criminal activities. Effective crime reporting systems improve transparency, accountability, and public participation in security management. Traditionally, crime reports were submitted through physical visits to police stations, telephone calls, written statements, and community meetings. Although these methods remain relevant, they are often associated with delays, inaccuracies, and difficulties in information management (Johnson, 2020). The rapid advancement of information technology has led to the development of digital crime reporting systems that utilize mobile applications, websites, cloud computing platforms, and database technologies to streamline reporting processes. Such systems provide faster communication channels and enable law enforcement agencies to manage reports more effectively.
Modern crime reporting systems often include features such as:
•	User registration and authentication. 
•	Digital incident reporting. 
•	Multimedia evidence submission. 
•	Automated notifications. 
•	Location tracking. 
•	Case management. 
•	Report monitoring. 
The proposed system incorporates these features to improve crime reporting efficiency within Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division.
2.3 Emergency Response Systems
Emergency response systems are technological and organizational frameworks designed to facilitate rapid action during emergencies. These systems coordinate communication, resource allocation, and operational activities to reduce the impact of incidents and protect lives and property. (Harris, 2023) defines emergency response systems as integrated communication and information management platforms that support decision-making and rapid deployment of emergency services. Emergency response systems are widely used in sectors such as healthcare, disaster management, transportation, firefighting, and law enforcement. In policing, emergency response systems help officers receive alerts, identify incident locations, dispatch personnel, and monitor ongoing situations. According to (Ahmed, 2022), the effectiveness of emergency response operations depends significantly on the speed and accuracy with which information is transmitted from victims or witnesses to responders. Traditional emergency response methods frequently suffer from communication delays, incomplete information, and inaccurate location descriptions. These challenges can reduce operational effectiveness and increase response times. The integration of digital technologies into emergency management systems has significantly improved communication efficiency and coordination. Features commonly found in modern emergency response systems include:
•	Emergency alert notifications. 
•	Incident tracking. 
•	GPS location sharing. 
•	Resource dispatch management. 
•	Communication dashboards. 
•	Real-time monitoring. 
The proposed system adopts these capabilities to enhance emergency response effectiveness within the study area.
2.4 Real-Time Location Tracking Technologies
Real-time location tracking refers to the continuous monitoring of the geographic position of a person, object, or device using technological systems such as GPS, cellular networks, wireless sensors, and internet-based communication platforms. (Clark, 2022) describes real-time tracking technologies as systems capable of collecting, transmitting, and processing geographic coordinates instantly to facilitate monitoring and decision-making activities. These technologies have become increasingly important in logistics, transportation, healthcare, security, and emergency response operations. The Global Positioning System (GPS) remains the most widely used location-tracking technology. GPS utilizes satellite signals to determine the precise location of a device anywhere on Earth. Modern smartphones incorporate GPS modules that enable mobile applications to access location data with high accuracy.(Garcia, 2021) explains that location-based services (LBS) combine GPS technologies, internet connectivity, and geographic information systems to deliver location-specific information and services to users.
The application of real-time location tracking in emergency response offers several advantages:
•	Faster identification of incident locations. 
•	Improved route planning. 
•	Enhanced resource deployment. 
•	Increased situational awareness. 
•	Reduced emergency response times. 
For law enforcement agencies, location tracking technology enables officers to locate victims quickly, identify crime hotspots, and coordinate response operations more efficiently.
The proposed system utilizes Expo Location API to capture and transmit user location coordinates during emergency reporting processes.
2.5 Mobile Application Technologies
Mobile applications have transformed how organizations deliver services and interact with users. The widespread availability of smartphones has created opportunities for developing innovative solutions across various sectors, including public safety and law enforcement.
According to (Adams, 2021), mobile applications provide convenient, accessible, and cost-effective platforms for information exchange and service delivery. The development of cross-platform frameworks such as React Native has further simplified mobile application development by allowing developers to build applications for multiple operating systems using a single codebase. React Native, developed by Meta Platforms, is widely recognized for its efficiency, performance, and flexibility in building mobile applications. The framework enables developers to create native-like user experiences while reducing development costs and time. The proposed system utilizes Expo Framework, an extension of React Native that simplifies development through built-in tools and APIs. Expo provides support for location services, secure storage, notifications, and device integration. TypeScript is employed as the primary programming language because it offers static typing, improved code quality, and enhanced maintainability (Lee, 2022). TypeScript helps developers identify errors during development, thereby improving software reliability.
The frontend technologies adopted for the proposed system include:
•	Expo (React Native) 
•	Expo Router 
•	TypeScript 
•	Axios 
•	Expo Location 
•	Expo Secure Store 
•	Expo Clipboard 
•	Ionicons 
These technologies collectively support the development of a robust, scalable, and user-friendly mobile application.
2.6 Review of Related Studies
Several researchers have investigated the application of information technology in crime management and emergency response systems. (Edwards, 2021) examined the role of digital technologies in modern policing and concluded that mobile-based reporting systems significantly improve citizen engagement and operational efficiency. The study found that digital platforms reduce reporting delays and enhance communication between law enforcement agencies and the public. (Evans, 2024) investigated crime information management systems and observed that centralized databases improve data accuracy, retrieval efficiency, and decision-making processes. The study recommended the adoption of cloud-based solutions for law enforcement organizations.
(Young, 2021) conducted research on mobile geographic information systems and emergency services. The findings indicated that GPS-enabled mobile applications improve emergency response effectiveness by providing accurate location information. (Franklin, 2022) explored secure authentication mechanisms for web and mobile applications. The study identified JSON Web Token (JWT) as an effective solution for managing authentication and authorization processes in distributed systems. Nelson (2021) examined technology management within law enforcement organizations and concluded that integrating mobile technologies into policing operations enhances service delivery and public trust. (Martinez, 2023) investigated human-computer interaction principles in mobile applications. The study emphasized the importance of user-friendly interfaces in promoting technology adoption and user satisfaction. These studies collectively demonstrate the importance of mobile technologies, secure authentication, location services, and centralized databases in modern crime management systems.
2.7 Existing System Review
Existing crime reporting systems can be categorized into traditional systems and digital systems.
Traditional Crime Reporting Systems
Traditional systems involve manual reporting through physical visits, written documentation, and telephone communication. While these methods have served law enforcement agencies for decades, they suffer from several limitations including:
•	Delayed reporting. 
•	Poor record management. 
•	Inaccurate location descriptions. 
•	Limited accessibility. 
•	Difficult data retrieval. 
According to (Johnson, 2020), traditional reporting systems are increasingly inadequate for addressing the operational demands of modern policing environments.
Digital Crime Reporting Systems
Digital systems utilize mobile applications, websites, and cloud platforms to facilitate crime reporting and incident management. These systems offer advantages such as:
•	Instant reporting. 
•	Real-time communication. 
•	Automated notifications. 
•	Digital record storage. 
•	Improved accessibility. 
Despite these benefits, many existing digital solutions lack integrated real-time location tracking and emergency response coordination features, creating opportunities for improvement. The proposed system addresses these shortcomings by combining crime reporting, emergency response management, GPS tracking, and secure authentication within a unified platform.
2.8 Theoretical Framework
This study is anchored on the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM).
The Technology Acceptance Model was developed by Davis in 1989 to explain factors influencing user acceptance of technology systems. The model suggests that two primary factors determine whether users adopt a technological solution:
1.	Perceived Usefulness. 
2.	Perceived Ease of Use. 
According to (Ibrahim, 2022), systems that users perceive as useful and easy to operate are more likely to achieve widespread adoption. The proposed Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System is designed to be simple, intuitive, and effective. Citizens can submit reports quickly while police administrators can manage incidents efficiently. These characteristics align with the principles of TAM and support user acceptance.
2.9 Gap in Literature
Although previous studies have examined crime reporting systems, emergency response technologies, mobile applications, and location-based services independently, limited research has focused on integrating all these components into a single platform specifically designed for local police operations in developing communities. Many existing systems emphasize crime documentation but lack real-time location tracking capabilities. Others focus on emergency response but do not provide comprehensive crime management functionalities.
Furthermore, limited attention has been given to implementing such systems using modern cross-platform technologies such as Expo React Native, TypeScript, Node.js, Express.js, MongoDB, and JWT authentication. This study addresses these gaps by developing a comprehensive mobile-based solution that combines crime reporting, emergency response coordination, GPS location tracking, and secure user authentication for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division.
2.10 Summary of Literature Review
This chapter reviewed literature related to crime reporting systems, emergency response systems, real-time location tracking technologies, mobile application technologies, related studies, and theoretical foundations supporting the proposed system. The review revealed that digital technologies have significantly improved public safety operations through enhanced communication, location awareness, and information management. It also identified limitations in existing systems, particularly regarding integration, accessibility, and real-time tracking capabilities. The findings justify the development of the proposed Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division. The next chapter presents the analysis and design of the proposed system.








CHAPTER THREE
3.1 SYSTEM ANALYSIS AND DESIGN
This chapter presents the analysis and design of the proposed Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division. System analysis involves studying the existing methods of crime reporting and emergency management to identify challenges and determine requirements for a more efficient solution. System design focuses on creating a blueprint for the proposed system, including its architecture, database structure, user interactions, and security mechanisms. The objective of this chapter is to provide a detailed description of the proposed system's structure and operational framework. The chapter covers the analysis of the existing system, identification of its weaknesses, analysis of the proposed system, system requirements, feasibility study, architectural design, use case design, database design, and security considerations. According to Ibrahim (2022), effective system analysis and design are essential for developing information systems that meet user requirements while ensuring reliability, scalability, and maintainability. Similarly, Jackson (2021) emphasizes that proper planning and design reduce implementation risks and improve software quality.
3.2 Analysis of the Existing System
The existing crime reporting process at many police stations, including Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division, is largely manual. Citizens typically report incidents through one of the following methods:
•	Physical visits to the police station. 
•	Telephone calls. 
•	Written complaints. 
•	Verbal reports to police officers. 
After receiving a report, officers manually document the details in registers, logbooks, or paper files. Information is then processed and forwarded to relevant departments for investigation or emergency response.
Existing System Workflow
1.	Incident occurs. 
2.	Citizen visits the police station or places a phone call. 
3.	Police officer records the complaint manually. 
4.	Information is reviewed. 
5.	Officers are assigned to the case. 
6.	Response team is dispatched. 
7.	Report is archived manually. 
Although this process has been used for many years, it presents several operational challenges.
3.3 Problems of the Existing System
The existing system suffers from several limitations that affect the efficiency of crime reporting and emergency response operations.
3.3.1 Delayed Reporting
Victims are often required to travel to police stations before submitting complaints. This can lead to significant delays, especially during emergencies.
3.3.2 Lack of Real-Time Location Information
Traditional reporting methods depend heavily on verbal descriptions of locations. Such descriptions may be inaccurate, incomplete, or difficult to interpret, resulting in delays in locating incidents.
Clark (2022) notes that accurate location information is critical for improving emergency response effectiveness and reducing response times.
3.3.3 Poor Record Management
Manual documentation creates challenges such as:
•	Misplaced records. 
•	Data duplication. 
•	Incomplete information. 
•	Difficult record retrieval. 
Green (2020) states that manual data management systems often experience inefficiencies that negatively affect organizational performance.
3.3.4 Communication Challenges
Citizens may find it difficult to obtain updates regarding submitted reports. Similarly, officers may struggle to communicate effectively with complainants during ongoing investigations.


3.3.5 Limited Accessibility
Reporting crimes is restricted by location and office hours in some cases, limiting citizens' ability to communicate with law enforcement agencies promptly.
3.3.6 Lack of Data Analytics
Manual systems provide limited opportunities for analyzing crime patterns, trends, and incident statistics that could support decision-making.
3.4 Analysis of the Proposed System
The proposed Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking is designed to overcome the limitations identified in the existing system.
The system provides a mobile platform through which citizens can:
•	Register and log in securely. 
•	Submit crime reports. 
•	Send emergency alerts. 
•	Share real-time GPS location. 
•	Track submitted reports. 
•	Receive notifications and updates. 
Police administrators can:
•	View reports. 
•	Monitor incidents. 
•	Access location information. 
•	Manage users. 
•	Assign response teams. 
•	Generate incident records. 
The proposed solution leverages modern technologies including:
Frontend Technologies
•	Expo (React Native) 
•	TypeScript 
•	Expo Router 
•	Expo Location 
•	Axios 
•	Expo Secure Store 

Backend Technologies
•	Node.js 
•	Express.js 
•	MongoDB (Mongoose) 
Security Technologies
•	JWT Authentication 
•	Secure Token Storage 
Deployment Platform
•	Railway Cloud Platform 
According to Adams (2021), mobile applications significantly improve service accessibility by enabling users to interact with systems regardless of their geographic location.
3.5 System Requirements
