4.9 Authentication and Security
System security was implemented using JSON Web Tokens (JWT).
JWT provides secure authentication by issuing encrypted access tokens after successful login. User passwords are hashed before being stored in MongoDB.
Authentication workflow:
1.	User registers. 
2.	Password is encrypted. 
3.	User logs in. 
4.	JWT token is generated. 
5.	Token is stored securely using Expo Secure Store. 
6.	Token is attached to API requests. 
7.	Backend verifies the token before granting access. 
Franklin (2022) recommends token-based authentication for distributed web and mobile systems because it improves security and scalability.
The system also implements Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) with two user roles:
•	Citizen 
•	Administrator 
This ensures users only access features appropriate to their roles.
4.10 Real-Time Location Tracking
One of the key features of the application is real-time location tracking. The Expo Location API retrieves the user's current GPS coordinates whenever a crime report or emergency alert is submitted.
Captured coordinates include:
•	Latitude 
•	Longitude 
•	Timestamp 
The coordinates are transmitted securely to the backend, stored in MongoDB, and displayed to administrators for faster incident response. According to Garcia (2021), integrating GPS technology into emergency response systems improves dispatch accuracy and reduces response time.
4.11 Railway Deployment
The backend application was deployed using the Railway cloud platform.
Railway was selected because it offers:
•	Automatic deployment 
•	Continuous integration 
•	Secure environment variable management 
•	Cloud database connectivity 
•	HTTPS support 
Deployment on Railway enables the application to be accessible over the internet, facilitating communication between the mobile client and the backend server.
