CHAPTER FIVE
5.1 SUMMARY, CONCLUSION AND RECOMMENDATIONS
This chapter presents the summary of the study, conclusion, recommendations, contributions to knowledge, suggestions for further studies, and the limitations encountered during the development of the Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division. The study was undertaken to address the challenges associated with traditional crime reporting methods, including delayed reporting, poor communication, manual record keeping, and the absence of real-time location tracking. The developed system demonstrates how modern mobile and web technologies can improve the efficiency of law enforcement operations and emergency response services.
5.2 Summary of the Study
Crime remains a major concern in many communities, and the effectiveness of law enforcement agencies depends largely on timely access to accurate information. Traditional crime reporting methods often involve physical visits to police stations or telephone calls, which may lead to delays, incomplete information, and poor record management. These limitations affect the ability of security agencies to respond promptly to emergencies and investigate reported incidents effectively. The aim of this project was to design and implement a Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division.
To achieve this aim, the study:
•	Examined the existing crime reporting process and identified its shortcomings. 
•	Reviewed relevant literature on crime reporting systems, emergency response technologies, mobile application development, and location-based services. 
•	Designed a secure and scalable system architecture. 
•	Developed a mobile application using Expo (React Native) and TypeScript. 
•	Implemented the backend using Node.js, Express.js, and MongoDB. 
•	Integrated JWT Authentication to secure user accounts and sessions. 
•	Utilized the Expo Location API to capture and transmit real-time GPS coordinates. 
•	Deployed the backend using the Railway Cloud Platform. 
•	Conducted functional and user acceptance testing to verify that the application met its objectives. 
The developed system provides an efficient digital platform through which citizens can report crimes, send emergency alerts, and share their real-time locations with police officers. Administrators can monitor incidents, manage users, update report statuses, and maintain digital records of reported cases. The implementation results demonstrate that the proposed system improves communication between citizens and law enforcement agencies, enhances response coordination, and supports better crime information management.
5.3 Conclusion
This study successfully designed and implemented a Crime Reporting and Emergency Response System with Real-Time Location Tracking for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division. The findings indicate that the integration of mobile computing technologies, cloud databases, and GPS location services can significantly improve crime reporting processes and emergency response management. The developed application addresses many of the shortcomings associated with traditional manual reporting methods by providing a secure, user-friendly, and location-aware platform. The use of Expo (React Native) enabled the development of a cross-platform mobile application with a consistent user experience, while Node.js, Express.js, and MongoDB provided a reliable backend infrastructure capable of managing user authentication, incident reports, and administrative operations. The integration of JWT authentication and secure token storage enhanced system security by ensuring that only authorized users could access protected resources. In addition, the implementation of real-time GPS tracking improved the accuracy of incident location identification, enabling faster response by law enforcement personnel. Overall, the study demonstrates that digital transformation within policing can improve operational efficiency, strengthen collaboration between citizens and security agencies, and contribute to public safety. If adopted and expanded, the proposed system has the potential to support more effective crime management and emergency response within Auchi Division and other police commands.
5.4 Recommendations
Based on the findings of this study, the following recommendations are made:
1.	Adoption by Law Enforcement Agencies: Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division, should consider adopting the proposed system to improve crime reporting, record management, and emergency response. 
2.	Public Awareness Campaigns: Awareness programs should be organized to educate citizens on how to use the application effectively and encourage timely reporting of criminal activities. 
3.	Integration with National Systems: Future versions of the application should integrate with national crime databases and emergency communication systems to improve information sharing among security agencies. 
4.	Offline Reporting Capability: An offline mode should be implemented to allow users to save reports when internet connectivity is unavailable and automatically synchronize them once a connection is restored. 
5.	Push Notifications: Future versions should include push notifications to provide users with updates on the status of their reports and emergency alerts. 
6.	Artificial Intelligence Integration: Artificial intelligence and machine learning techniques could be incorporated to identify crime patterns, predict crime hotspots, and assist decision-making. 
7.	GIS Mapping: The system should integrate Geographic Information System (GIS) mapping to visualize crime incidents geographically and support strategic deployment of law enforcement resources. 
8.	Multi-Language Support: Future implementations should support multiple local languages to improve accessibility for users with diverse linguistic backgrounds. 
9.	Continuous Security Updates: Regular security assessments and updates should be conducted to protect user information and maintain system integrity. 
10.	Training of Police Personnel: Officers should receive adequate training on the use of digital crime reporting systems to maximize the benefits of the technology. 
5.5 Contributions to Knowledge
This study contributes to knowledge in several ways:
•	It demonstrates the practical application of Expo (React Native) and TypeScript in developing a mobile-based public safety application. 
•	It integrates real-time GPS location tracking into a crime reporting and emergency response system tailored to the operational needs of a local police command. 
•	It applies JWT-based authentication and role-based access control to enhance system security. 
•	It provides a scalable system architecture using Node.js, Express.js, and MongoDB. 
•	It serves as a reference for future research on mobile applications for public safety, emergency management, and smart policing. 
5.6 Suggestions for Further Studies
Future researchers may extend this work by exploring the following areas:
•	Integration of live chat and voice communication between citizens and police officers. 
•	Incorporation of image, video, and audio evidence uploads with advanced verification mechanisms. 
•	Deployment of the system across multiple police divisions with centralized administration. 
•	Integration with ambulance, fire service, and other emergency response agencies. 
•	Development of predictive crime analytics using artificial intelligence and machine learning. 
•	Evaluation of the system's impact on response times and public trust through long-term field studies. 
•	Adoption of blockchain technology for secure and tamper-resistant crime records. 
5.7 Limitations of the Study
Although the objectives of the study were achieved, several limitations were encountered:
•	The system depends on internet connectivity for real-time communication. 
•	GPS accuracy may be affected by environmental conditions or device limitations. 
•	The project was developed within a limited timeframe, restricting large-scale field testing. 
•	The application was designed specifically for Area Command Police Station, Auchi Division, and was not integrated with national law enforcement databases. 
•	Resource constraints limited the inclusion of advanced features such as predictive analytics and GIS visualization. 
Despite these limitations, the developed system provides a practical foundation for enhancing crime reporting and emergency response through mobile technology.



